From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:43:56 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 65/75] wasm32: register variables (cs_opt_regvar at -O2 and
 above)

wasm32 had no register variables: every local, parameter and function
result lived in the linear-memory shadow stack. Enable cs_opt_regvar
(supported switch, part of -O2) and fix what that exposes:

- hlcgobj gen_load_para_value / gen_load_cgpara_loc (thlcgobj, used by
  the high-level code generator targets; thlcg2ll targets such as x86
  have their own overrides): handle parameters whose initial location
  is a register variable (LOC_CREGISTER/LOC_CFPUREGISTER) by loading the
  incoming parameter into a register and making it the variable's
  location, as thlcg2ll does.
- Narrow unsigned parameters (wasm32 only): callers pass 8/16-bit values
  sign-extended (the parameter location's def is the signed type). A
  memory copy normalised them through its narrow store and load, a
  register copy does not, so e.g. a Char/Byte/Word parameter >= $80
  compared wrong (RTL Pos() with characters >= $80). Normalise with
  a_load_reg_reg from the parameter location's def to the variable's
  def.
- Loop back-edge liveness (nwasmflw twasmwhilerepeatnode): the wasm
  register allocator uses linear live ranges, so the wasm local of a
  register variable last used before the end of a loop body could be
  reused later in the body while the next iteration still needed it
  (miscompiled for loop). sync_regvars around the loop keeps the
  variables used in the loop live from before the loop to its end; the
  wasm rgcpu drops the ra_sync markers afterwards.
- symsym tabstractnormalvarsym.setregable (wasm32 only): no register
  variables for externref/funcref values (their own kind of wasm local),
  and none for non-ordinal, non-float values larger than 4 bytes (method
  pointers, 8-byte records): wasm32 has no register pairs (reghi is
  NR_NO on cpuhighleveltarget) and such values are accessed as two
  32-bit halves, which ended in "local.get INVALID" / a compiler AV.
- Typecasts on the assignment side (htypechk valid_for_assign, wasm32
  as for llvm): the high level code generator copies a register variable
  into a new register for a value typecast (ncgcnv second_int_to_int,
  cpuhighleveltarget), so an assignment such as "byte(e) := 3" to a
  register variable was lost (FPC testsuite webtbs/tw4809: no range
  check error at -O2). Such variables are now not regable, as on llvm.

Procedures that use exceptions keep their variables in memory (generic
rule); see the next commit.
